From 7f2fe7429726f40efa8aeddc6e3644f9ef7bed38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude-checkpoint <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 02:38:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] interpreter: read and write memory words by their
 alignment on riscv64

MLOAD, MSTORE and CALLDATALOAD move a 32-byte big-endian word between
memory and the stack. riscv64im has neither misaligned loads nor a
byte swap, so byte by byte that is 32 loads or stores, each with its
shift.

At an address that is a multiple of 8 a word is four loads or stores
and the reversal of each limb. At a multiple of 4 it is eight, and
with the half from the lower address in the upper half of the limb,
only the bytes of each half are left to reverse. A limb that is zero
is not reversed. Other addresses take the path they took.

The reversal is the three stages of masks and shifts, 13 instructions,
in assembly: written in Rust, LLVM recognises the stages and puts its
own lowering of a byte swap in their place, an or-tree of the shifted
bytes (21 to 23 instructions, as measured in our revm fork, where this
comes from). The masks are loaded from memory.

For a word of EVM memory to lie at a multiple of 8, the buffer has to
start at one. RSP's guest allocator aligns every block so.

Instructions a block in a Pico guest of RSP, before and after:
  13 mainnet blocks   157.522 M -> 155.667 M  (-1.18 %)
  3 Sepolia blocks    432.535 M -> 430.330 M  (-0.51 %)
  45 Sepolia blocks   539.710 M -> 536.225 M  (-0.65 %)
The guests commit the same values on all 61 blocks. The gain is held
back by MSTORE, whose instruction is no longer inlined into its
handler; the next commit takes that up.

Tests: a word read and written at 16 consecutive addresses, for 252
words of every shape the paths tell apart, against from_be_bytes and
to_be_bytes, with the bytes beside it checked; the two stages of the
reversal against swap_bytes; CALLDATALOAD's padding past the end of
the data. On a host these run the same paths with the stages in Rust.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
